00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:07,290
He's not listening.

250
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:12,610
He's not reading the page and seeing what it says about the volume of the music.

251
00:12:13,010 --> 00:12:19,230
So basically the dynamics are like the instructions of how loud to play it or not so loud.

252
00:12:19,390 --> 00:12:19,750
Exactly.

253
00:12:20,130 --> 00:12:22,390
So does it say pianissimo or the opposite?

254
00:12:23,050 --> 00:12:24,090
Does it say forte?

255
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:27,240
And it will actually say this on the notes.

256
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:28,240
Yes.

257
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:29,090
Forte, it'll say that.

258
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:31,080
F-O-R-T-E or sometimes just F.

259
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:32,080
Oh, okay.

260
00:12:32,290 --> 00:12:34,030
So P is piano.

261
00:12:34,050 --> 00:12:35,830
That means like we said earlier, quiet.

262
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:39,280
Pianissimo is P, P, P. It's very, very quiet.

263
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:40,750
F is forte, loud.

264
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:42,560
Okay.

265
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:52,210
So I think, well, if anyone out there plays an instrument you can relate and you can maybe know about these things that you may know it in your original language.

266
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:59,150
I know many people who play musical instruments and don't really know the technical words in their original language.

267
00:12:59,350 --> 00:13:02,990
I mean, things like Andante and pianissimo, things like this, I think are universal, right?

268
00:13:03,090 --> 00:13:04,490
They're used in whatever language.

269
00:13:04,670 --> 00:13:04,890
Exactly.

270
00:13:05,840 --> 00:13:06,960
Very, very widely used.

271
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:16,360
But things like the accidentals or bass clef or scales and stuff like that, I think it's obviously different languages.

272
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:20,080
For example, you know Italian, like scales or accidentals, are they?

273
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:20,510
They're different.

274
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:21,310
They're different, right?

275
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,080
They're different.

276
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:24,080
All of the other words.

277
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:32,680
So the instructions on the page, because music is shared all over the world, the instructions on the page are almost always in Italian, so piano, andante, or they're in German.

278
00:13:33,160 --> 00:13:34,160
Oh, really?

279
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:35,160
Some music.

280
00:13:35,160 --> 00:13:39,520
And everything else, scales, octaves, these are all in your native language.

281
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:44,440
And so they're very, very important to study if you want to play and I think very, very useful if you want to study an instrument.

282
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:44,880
Yeah, of course.

283
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:59,040
I think it's also a very good idea to just have a general knowledge about these things because sometimes it's nice to know a little bit about it and maybe if a friend is talking about music, you can maybe understand a little bit more about what it's like.

284
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:05,760
Or if you see music, a sheet of music, you can now more or less know what's going on with all those drawings and little scribbles there.

285
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:06,760
Absolutely.

